	<item>
		<title>HTML5: Font Profiling Project</title>
		<link>https://galactic.ink/journal/?p=213</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 29 Jan 2011 06:14:21 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://mudcu.be/journal/?p=213</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[The <em><strong>Font Profiling Project</strong></em> gathers statistics on font-family support in web browsers. Information on your computer's font support, remote address, user agent and processing speed will be submitted to the server when the test completes — an aggregate of the information will be published detailing the current state of font-family support on the web.

The data generated from your computer will help profile the state of font-face support on the web. Your computers font-face support, remote address, user agent and processing time will be submitted to the server upon completion of the test — a statistic analysis of the data will be published — no specific information about your computer will be published.

Take the <a href="http://mudcu.be/labs/fpp/">Font Profiling Test</a>.

Here's a run-down of my recent Typographic projects using the &lt;canvas&gt; element:
<ol>
	<li>Take the Unicode Acid Test:
<a href="http://mudcu.be/journal/2010/11/unicode-profiling-project/">http://mudcu.be/journal/2010/11/unicode-profiling-project/</a></li>
	<li>Learn about tying text-support into your &lt;canvas&gt; application:
<a href="http://mudcu.be/journal/html5-typographic-metrics/">http://mudcu.be/journal/html5-typographic-metrics/</a></li>
	<li>See demos of CSS metrics being used in &lt;canvas&gt;:
<a href="http://mudcu.be/labs/Typography/metrics.html">http://mudcu.be/labs/Typography/metrics.html</a></li>
</ol>]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[]]></excerpt:encoded>
