			<wp:comment_date_gmt><![CDATA[2012-11-15 17:00:00]]></wp:comment_date_gmt>
			<wp:comment_content><![CDATA[Hi, my family just found your app. Thanks for it, as the 4 year old and 11 year old have been using it as a player and it creates a lot of interest in the classical music. I wonder if there is another free app out there like in a kids' arcade or something that already has a computer keyboard to piano keyboard config. you could use? I will think about it. I am interested in trying to hook it up to my Casio WK 200 keyboard, or finding another cheap midi controller keyboard to play that will work with your program. 

Piano people are used to having their music above their keyboard . If I was trying to play off a keyboard along with your program, to learn to play, I think I would want the bars that haven't been played yet to float down, where they come in contact with the piano to be played.  I never played guitar hero, so those players might be used to doing it this way.

I am trying to think how else it could be used. Maybe it could stop and wait for you to play each note in a certain mode? I can get my sister and sister in law who specialize in piano education to give feedback. A way to tell where you are on the score might be helpful, so an advanced student with a tricky piece could work on a certain part.

As far as a learning tool, the problem you run into in synesthesia sometimes is that the colors are too similar to each other to distinguish.  In theory  you would think you would want the sharps and flats to be related on the color  wheel, but if you try to play them, it's hard to tell which color to play. I have been working with Annie Lang's Rainbow music book for a year, and even in good light it is hard to tell magenta from red orange right next to each other, and she uses a very simple system with just a few flats and sharps. It can get a little interesting trying to work out color names for some of the unusual designer colors as well. I was calling out "Lavender, olive, horsey-color, dirt, mauve" to my 3 year old and trying to get her to play it. So I am interested in finding or making a new system. I am thinking that starting with easier music and dumbed down scales with fewer and contrasting colors is the way to go, like "synesthesia for dummies".  We need to start with the easiest piano pieces, too. Nursery rhymes and folk songs. One thing great about the Annie Lang books, is that I can sight read through all of the pieces easily without nervousness at the correct speed. I think that someone could build a really good learning system based on synesthesia with this technology. I guess one thing that might need an artist's touch is not just how the colors look on the piano keyboard, but how they are likely to look on paper and on the music being played in your system. But the thing to realize, is it is just a certain range that is being played, especially in beginning to intermediate pieces. I bet the other notes are mostly part of a pattern, such as Grieg's Concerto in A minor, I believe, where a pattern goes from the bottom to the top of the keyboard. Some of your pieces get a bug where the notes keep the color until the end of the piece. You can see that especially in the strictly classical pieces, there is not that great of a range. Maybe that would be a way to find the easier pieces on your list automatically, by finding those that have the majority of notes within a smaller range. Then, another way to vastly help students would be to let you transpose the pieces into an easier key like C or G, that could have a more simple synesthesia without all the sharps and flats. A synesthesia that is adapted to each key or piece might be an idea, so only notes that are going to be used in each piece would need colors. 

One thing about these songs, is, I don't understand how midi songs can have such great depth of expression. These are all really beautiful songs.]]></wp:comment_content>
