			<wp:comment_date_gmt><![CDATA[2012-11-15 17:35:12]]></wp:comment_date_gmt>
			<wp:comment_content><![CDATA[For those who can't wait to play something that makes sense, I found that playing Q and Z together make an A octave. W and X make the next note in the A major scale, and it goes up through the A major scale. As long as you don't mind playing in A major, you ought to have at least have as much fun as a kid with a toy xylophone (and that means a lot of fun.) You can play Joy to the world, for sure. You can find the chromatic keys too. Playing this way is a little like playing a chromatic dulcimer. It can be done, but it's not too easy. You could probably find I certain note you needed though for a song. I managed to find all the lower notes between 1-Q and  -Backspace. The semicolon note hits middle C. They mostly go up between qwerty and the #s. That might give people a clue to get them started until the bug gets fixed.]]></wp:comment_content>
